# Repo-wide default: git stores text as LF and materializes it as LF in the
# working tree on EVERY platform. This tree is shared between Windows-side and
# WSL sessions over the same directory, and a Windows tool rewrote the whole
# tree to CRLF -- 1044 files -- which breaks `bash` outright: it reads the \r as
# part of each token, so .sh scripts die with "$'\r': command not found" and
# .githooks/pre-commit stops working. `eol=lf`, not just `text=auto`, is what
# pins the WORKING TREE; text=auto alone governs only what gets committed, and
# the working tree was the half that was actually broken.
#
# Binary files are excluded automatically by text=auto's content detection.
# The four .cmd files are already stored LF and work that way, so they are
# deliberately NOT carved out to eol=crlf -- that would be an unrequested
# change to files nobody reported a problem with.
#
# Verified per the fresh-clone rule below: clones at core.autocrlf=true AND
# =false both yield 1092 w/lf, 0 w/crlf, and zero CR in
# plugins/bootstrap/scripts/bootstrap-reset-cooldown.sh.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Workflow tool scripts must be LF-only. The Claude Code Workflow tool's
# permission layer rejects launching a scriptPath whose content contains
# control characters ("script contains control characters that would be hidden
# in the approval dialog") -- a CR (\r) counts. With core.autocrlf=true a
# Windows checkout would otherwise rewrite these LF blobs to CRLF in the working
# tree (and in the marketplace-synced plugin cache), tripping that rejection.
# eol=lf pins the working-tree bytes to LF regardless of the user's autocrlf.
#
# TWO patterns, because workflow scripts ship from two shapes: a skill's own
# workflow/ directory, and a plugin-level workflows/ directory. The second was
# added after content-pipeline-kit's run-ready-wave.js shipped CRLF in 0.14.0 and
# the Workflow tool refused to launch it -- the lane was unreachable on any
# Windows checkout with the default core.autocrlf=true. A new workflow script in
# a THIRD location would need a third pattern; check this file when adding one.
#
# `text` IS REQUIRED and is not redundant with `eol=lf`. The gitattributes docs
# say specifying eol "automatically sets text if text was left unspecified", and
# the first version of this file relied on exactly that sentence. It does not
# hold on git 2.55.0.windows.3: with `eol=lf` alone, `git check-attr` reports
# `text: unspecified` and a fresh `core.autocrlf=true` clone still checks the
# files out as CRLF -- 266 CR in run-ready-wave.js, 368 in claude-md-detect.js.
# Verified by cloning this repo into a scratch directory both ways; `text eol=lf`
# reports `text: set` and yields 0 CR. Do not "simplify" the `text` back out.
#
# VERIFY A CHANGE HERE WITH A FRESH CLONE, never with the working tree. This
# file governs CHECKOUT, so an already-materialized working-tree file keeps its
# old bytes and git still reports it clean -- the working tree cannot tell you
# whether a consumer would get LF.
plugins/skills-kit/skills/*/workflow/*.js text eol=lf
plugins/*/workflows/*.js text eol=lf
